1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,821
Welcome to part two.

2
00:00:01,821 --> 00:00:04,924
In part one we built and verified the platform:

3
00:00:04,924 --> 00:00:10,339
the network, the cluster, the load balancer controller, and a restricted workload.

4
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:16,931
Now we connect GitHub to AWS without any stored keys, and push real code through the pipeline.

5
00:00:16,931 --> 00:00:19,176
It goes live in under five minutes.

6
00:00:19,733 --> 00:00:21,645
Then we break things on purpose.

7
00:00:21,645 --> 00:00:24,751
A vulnerable dependency that the pipeline must stop,

8
00:00:24,751 --> 00:00:29,828
and a broken deployment that we troubleshoot, layer by layer, like you would at work.

9
00:00:30,367 --> 00:00:35,770
And at the end, we delete everything, and prove that nothing is left behind.

10
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:36,481
Let us go.

11
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:40,756
This is an important DevSecOps section.

12
00:00:40,756 --> 00:00:45,608
The usual way to let a pipeline into AWS is to store two secrets in GitHub:

13
00:00:45,608 --> 00:00:48,260
an access key ID and a secret access key.

14
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:54,729
They are long lived, they can be copied, and they stay valid until someone remembers to rotate them.

15
00:00:54,729 --> 00:00:56,735
We do not store either of them.

16
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:58,284
Instead, OIDC.

17
00:00:58,284 --> 00:01:03,131
For every job, GitHub signs a short token that says which repository,

18
00:01:03,131 --> 00:01:06,362
which branch and which environment is running.

19
00:01:06,362 --> 00:01:12,053
AWS checks that token and returns temporary credentials, valid for that job only.

20
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:13,367
The flow:

21
00:01:13,367 --> 00:01:17,631
the GitHub Actions job sends its token to AWS STS.

22
00:01:18,167 --> 00:01:21,090
If the token matches the role's trust policy,

23
00:01:21,090 --> 00:01:26,026
the job gets temporary credentials for the eks devsecops github deploy role.

24
00:01:26,567 --> 00:01:28,712
And that role can do very little:

25
00:01:28,712 --> 00:01:33,589
push to one container repository, and deploy into one Kubernetes namespace.

26
00:01:33,589 --> 00:01:36,450
The workflow receives temporary credentials.

27
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:40,351
We do not have long lived AWS secrets sitting inside GitHub.

28
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:47,052
Here is the real trust policy, recorded from IAM with the account number redacted.

29
00:01:47,052 --> 00:01:52,064
The principal is the account's shared GitHub identity provider, which we only read.

30
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:53,876
Condition one:

31
00:01:53,876 --> 00:01:57,249
the token's audience must be AWS STS.

32
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,759
Condition two, the important one:

33
00:01:59,759 --> 00:02:04,212
the subject must be this repository, running in its production environment.

34
00:02:04,212 --> 00:02:09,140
The production environment in GitHub only accepts deployments from the main branch.

35
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:13,415
So a pull request, a fork, or another branch can never assume this role.

36
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:15,994
Notice the numbers in the subject:

37
00:02:15,994 --> 00:02:20,048
GitHub's new immutable format includes the owner and repository IDs.

38
00:02:20,048 --> 00:02:26,249
If someone deleted this repository and created a new one with the same name, its tokens would not match.

39
00:02:26,249 --> 00:02:31,973
My first deploy in the original build actually failed on this, until I updated the trust policy.

40
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:34,494
And the permissions are tiny:

41
00:02:34,494 --> 00:02:39,429
log in to the registry, push to one repository, and describe one cluster.

42
00:02:39,429 --> 00:02:43,688
Inside Kubernetes, the access entry limits it to one namespace.

43
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:48,671
Let us walk through the pipeline, stage by stage.

44
00:02:48,671 --> 00:02:53,297
Stage one, secret scanning with gitleaks, across the full git history.

45
00:02:53,297 --> 00:03:00,500
Secrets must be found before anything is deployed, because a leaked key in git is public the moment you push.

46
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:02,085
Stage two, code quality:

47
00:03:02,085 --> 00:03:04,068
the linter and the unit tests.

48
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:06,721
Stage three, dependency security:

49
00:03:06,721 --> 00:03:11,150
npm audit fails on any high or critical vulnerability in our packages.

50
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:15,768
Stage four, hadolint, which checks the Dockerfile against best practices,

51
00:03:15,768 --> 00:03:18,678
like pinning versions and not running as root.

52
00:03:18,678 --> 00:03:25,321
In the same stage, actionlint and zizmor check our own workflows, and every action is pinned to a commit.

53
00:03:25,867 --> 00:03:28,087
Stage five, infrastructure security:

54
00:03:28,087 --> 00:03:33,392
Trivy scans the Terraform and the rendered Kubernetes manifests for misconfigurations,

55
00:03:33,392 --> 00:03:36,969
and kubeconform checks them against the Kubernetes schema.

56
00:03:36,969 --> 00:03:39,189
This is an important DevSecOps idea:

57
00:03:39,189 --> 00:03:42,088
we scan the infrastructure before we deploy it.

58
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,892
Stages six to nine:

59
00:03:43,892 --> 00:03:48,396
build the image, scan it, where critical and high block the release,

60
00:03:48,396 --> 00:03:51,708
generate the SBOM, and sign the image with cosign.

61
00:03:52,267 --> 00:03:53,511
Stages ten to twelve:

62
00:03:53,511 --> 00:03:57,185
push to ECR and deploy the exact digest, wait for the rollout,

63
00:03:57,185 --> 00:04:03,408
prove the pipeline's own permissions are limited, and smoke test the live site through the load balancer.

64
00:04:05,167 --> 00:04:07,823
Here is how that maps to the workflow.

65
00:04:07,823 --> 00:04:12,716
Job one, checks, runs the whole CI suite from the other workflow file.

66
00:04:13,267 --> 00:04:17,527
Job two builds and scans the image, but only if every check passed.

67
00:04:17,527 --> 00:04:20,006
And it has no cloud permissions at all:

68
00:04:20,006 --> 00:04:23,376
the scanning tools never run next to AWS credentials.

69
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:25,179
Job three deploys.

70
00:04:25,179 --> 00:04:28,501
It runs in the protected production environment,

71
00:04:28,501 --> 00:04:32,100
it is the only job allowed to request an OIDC token,

72
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:37,430
and it pushes, signs, applies, waits, checks its permissions and smoke tests.

73
00:04:37,967 --> 00:04:38,899
An honest note:

74
00:04:38,899 --> 00:04:41,634
while preparing this workshop I found a gap.

75
00:04:41,634 --> 00:04:44,555
The deploy workflow used to skip the CI checks,

76
00:04:44,555 --> 00:04:50,148
so a vulnerable dependency could have reached production as long as the image scan passed.

77
00:04:50,148 --> 00:04:51,329
Job one fixes that.

78
00:04:51,329 --> 00:04:53,691
You will see it work in a few minutes.

79
00:04:55,433 --> 00:04:57,897
One more concept before we push.

80
00:04:57,897 --> 00:05:01,592
A tag, like profile card 1.0.5, is just a label.

81
00:05:01,592 --> 00:05:07,828
In most registries, anyone who can push can move that label to different content.

82
00:05:08,367 --> 00:05:13,621
Our ECR repository has immutable tags, so a tag can never be overwritten.

83
00:05:14,167 --> 00:05:15,929
A digest is different:

84
00:05:15,929 --> 00:05:19,453
it is the SHA 256 hash of the image content.

85
00:05:19,453 --> 00:05:22,656
Change one byte, and the digest changes.

86
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,310
So the pipeline deploys the digest.

87
00:05:25,310 --> 00:05:31,520
The cluster runs exactly the bytes that were scanned and signed, and nothing can be swapped in between.

88
00:05:33,267 --> 00:05:34,942
Now the developer workflow.

89
00:05:34,942 --> 00:05:36,865
I connected the pipeline first:

90
00:05:36,865 --> 00:05:42,821
the deploy role from Terraform's output went into a GitHub secret on the production environment,

91
00:05:42,821 --> 00:05:45,985
never printed, and the deploy switch was turned on.

92
00:05:45,985 --> 00:05:47,970
Then a small, controlled change:

93
00:05:47,970 --> 00:05:51,010
add Amazon EKS to the skills on the profile card.

94
00:05:51,567 --> 00:05:53,942
git add, git commit, git push.

95
00:05:53,942 --> 00:05:56,397
That is all the developer does.

96
00:05:56,397 --> 00:05:59,406
Everything else is the platform's job.

97
00:06:01,167 --> 00:06:04,520
Seconds later, the deploy workflow starts on its own.

98
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:05,722
Here it is mid-run:

99
00:06:05,722 --> 00:06:10,721
the five security check jobs ran in parallel and passed in about half a minute,

100
00:06:10,721 --> 00:06:12,872
and build and scan is running now.

101
00:06:12,872 --> 00:06:14,138
Deploy waits for it.

102
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:17,019
The finished run.

103
00:06:17,019 --> 00:06:17,744
The checks:

104
00:06:17,744 --> 00:06:20,641
secret scan five seconds, app checks eleven,

105
00:06:20,641 --> 00:06:24,856
the container job twenty six, the infrastructure job thirty two,

106
00:06:24,856 --> 00:06:29,400
and the pipeline security job, which checks our own workflows, eight.

107
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:34,405
They run in parallel, so the whole security suite takes about half a minute.

108
00:06:34,967 --> 00:06:37,916
Build and scan, fifty one seconds.

109
00:06:38,467 --> 00:06:45,303
And deploy to EKS, three minutes, most of it waiting for the new pods to become healthy behind the load balancer.

110
00:06:45,867 --> 00:06:49,581
Four minutes and thirty six seconds from git push to live.

111
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:51,758
Let us look inside the deploy job.

112
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,112
Assuming role with OIDC, authenticated as a session named after the run.

113
00:06:58,112 --> 00:06:59,521
Notice the image line:

114
00:06:59,521 --> 00:07:05,093
GitHub prints three stars instead of the account number, because the workflow masks it.

115
00:07:05,093 --> 00:07:07,014
Our public logs never show it.

116
00:07:07,567 --> 00:07:12,423
ECR's own scan completed with zero findings, a second opinion after Trivy.

117
00:07:12,423 --> 00:07:16,098
Then cosign signed the image and verified the signature.

118
00:07:16,633 --> 00:07:20,815
kubectl applied our seven objects, and the rollout finished.

119
00:07:21,367 --> 00:07:24,245
Then the pipeline proves its own limits.

120
00:07:24,245 --> 00:07:27,986
Yes, it may create deployments in its own namespace.

121
00:07:27,986 --> 00:07:34,390
No to kube-system, no to secrets, no to cluster role bindings, no to deleting namespaces.

122
00:07:34,390 --> 00:07:38,131
If any of those answers ever changes, the job fails.

123
00:07:39,867 --> 00:07:41,116
kubectl get pods:

124
00:07:41,116 --> 00:07:46,261
two pods running, on different nodes, in different availability zones.

125
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,814
The service is a ClusterIP:

126
00:07:48,814 --> 00:07:52,395
an internal address only, not exposed by itself.

127
00:07:52,933 --> 00:07:54,714
And the Ingress has an address:

128
00:07:54,714 --> 00:07:59,368
the DNS name of the Application Load Balancer that the controller created for us.

129
00:07:59,933 --> 00:08:05,805
In AWS, the target group shows both pod IP addresses on port 8080, healthy.

130
00:08:05,805 --> 00:08:09,328
The load balancer talks straight to the pods.

131
00:08:11,067 --> 00:08:12,163
In the console:

132
00:08:12,163 --> 00:08:18,816
the load balancer, internet facing, of type application, active in both availability zones.

133
00:08:18,816 --> 00:08:20,863
Terraform did not create it.

134
00:08:20,863 --> 00:08:23,568
The controller did, from our Ingress.

135
00:08:24,133 --> 00:08:28,708
And its target group, with our two pods as targets, both healthy.

136
00:08:30,467 --> 00:08:32,322
And here it is in the browser:

137
00:08:32,322 --> 00:08:37,083
the profile card, with Amazon EKS now in the skills, served from our cluster.

138
00:08:37,083 --> 00:08:41,041
The footer shows the version and the commit of this exact build.

139
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:46,517
Let us trace the request from the internet all the way to the pod.

140
00:08:46,517 --> 00:08:49,840
The browser connects to the load balancer's public address.

141
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:53,966
The Application Load Balancer lives in the public subnets.

142
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:59,433
The Ingress rules, which the controller turned into load balancer rules, say:

143
00:08:59,433 --> 00:09:01,420
every path goes to our service.

144
00:09:01,967 --> 00:09:05,276
The service tells the controller which pods belong to it.

145
00:09:05,833 --> 00:09:08,343
The pods live in the private subnets.

146
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:14,725
And inside each pod, nginx serves the profile card files, with the security headers.

147
00:09:15,267 --> 00:09:20,888
Because we use target type IP, the load balancer sends traffic directly to the pod addresses,

148
00:09:20,888 --> 00:09:26,026
and our network policy lets exactly those load balancer subnets in, and nothing else.

149
00:09:27,767 --> 00:09:31,393
One detail I noticed in the recording, and it is worth knowing.

150
00:09:31,393 --> 00:09:35,250
On the very first deployment, the readiness gates column says none.

151
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:38,473
The pods were created at the same moment as the Ingress,

152
00:09:38,473 --> 00:09:42,848
before the controller knew about the service, so it could not attach a gate.

153
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:46,411
From the second rollout on, every pod gets a gate:

154
00:09:46,411 --> 00:09:50,385
it only counts as ready when the load balancer reports it healthy.

155
00:09:50,385 --> 00:09:53,275
That is what makes later rollouts zero downtime.

156
00:09:53,275 --> 00:09:56,647
In production, you deploy the Ingress and service first,

157
00:09:56,647 --> 00:10:00,080
or simply accept that the very first rollout has no gate.

158
00:10:01,833 --> 00:10:07,878
Now let us deliberately make the security scan fail, so you can see why this stage exists.

159
00:10:07,878 --> 00:10:11,706
Imagine a hurried developer adds lodash, version 4.17.20,

160
00:10:11,706 --> 00:10:15,333
for a few string helpers, and pushes straight to main.

161
00:10:15,333 --> 00:10:18,893
That version has known high severity vulnerabilities.

162
00:10:20,633 --> 00:10:21,698
The run is red.

163
00:10:21,698 --> 00:10:25,533
The app job failed, and build and deploy were skipped.

164
00:10:26,067 --> 00:10:27,327
The log shows why:

165
00:10:27,327 --> 00:10:33,841
npm audit found lodash with a command injection and a code injection advisory, severity high.

166
00:10:33,841 --> 00:10:34,821
Exit code one.

167
00:10:35,367 --> 00:10:40,469
Build and deploy never started, and the live site kept serving the previous version.

168
00:10:40,469 --> 00:10:42,230
This is exactly what we want.

169
00:10:42,230 --> 00:10:46,603
The pipeline failed, and that is actually a successful security outcome:

170
00:10:46,603 --> 00:10:49,336
the vulnerable code never reached production.

171
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:53,686
The fix is the normal developer loop.

172
00:10:53,686 --> 00:10:55,153
Reproduce it locally:

173
00:10:55,153 --> 00:10:57,459
the same audit, the same finding.

174
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,169
Upgrade to the patched version, 4.18.1, and the audit is clean.

175
00:11:03,733 --> 00:11:07,090
Commit with a message that names the advisory, and push.

176
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:10,267
All five checks pass, the image is built and scanned,

177
00:11:10,267 --> 00:11:14,583
and the deployment goes green in three minutes and twenty three seconds.

178
00:11:16,333 --> 00:11:17,917
Next, troubleshooting.

179
00:11:17,917 --> 00:11:21,372
When something breaks, do not start by guessing.

180
00:11:21,372 --> 00:11:25,403
First I check whether the problem is user facing at all:

181
00:11:25,403 --> 00:11:26,483
is the site up?

182
00:11:27,033 --> 00:11:29,021
Then I work through the layers:

183
00:11:29,021 --> 00:11:32,419
is it application level, does the process even start?

184
00:11:32,419 --> 00:11:35,818
Is it container level, does it answer inside the pod?

185
00:11:36,367 --> 00:11:38,622
Is it Kubernetes level:

186
00:11:38,622 --> 00:11:41,466
pods, rollout, events, ports?

187
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,454
Or is it at the AWS load balancer level:

188
00:11:44,454 --> 00:11:47,092
the Ingress backends and the target health?

189
00:11:47,092 --> 00:11:51,325
Each layer has its command, and the evidence tells you where to stop.

190
00:11:53,067 --> 00:11:55,163
A lesson before the real incident.

191
00:11:55,163 --> 00:11:59,911
My first attempt to break the rollout was a typo in the readiness probe path:

192
00:11:59,911 --> 00:12:01,515
health instead of healthz.

193
00:12:01,515 --> 00:12:02,933
And the rollout passed!

194
00:12:03,467 --> 00:12:04,368
Here is why.

195
00:12:04,368 --> 00:12:06,997
Our nginx serves a single page app:

196
00:12:06,997 --> 00:12:11,204
any unknown path returns index dot html with status 200.

197
00:12:11,204 --> 00:12:14,359
So the typo path looked perfectly healthy.

198
00:12:14,359 --> 00:12:16,763
Only healthz returns plain text.

199
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:17,998
The lesson:

200
00:12:17,998 --> 00:12:22,438
on single page apps, health probes must point at a dedicated endpoint,

201
00:12:22,438 --> 00:12:25,800
and you should test that a wrong path actually fails.

202
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:28,655
I reverted the change, and broke it properly.

203
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:31,535
The real incident:

204
00:12:31,535 --> 00:12:37,145
someone changes the readiness probe to port 80, thinking of the service port, and pushes.

205
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:39,035
Layer one, users:

206
00:12:39,035 --> 00:12:41,782
the site still answers, status 200.

207
00:12:41,782 --> 00:12:44,216
Good, so this is not an outage.

208
00:12:44,216 --> 00:12:45,315
Then the pods:

209
00:12:45,315 --> 00:12:52,301
two old pods ready, and one new pod at zero of one, with its readiness gate also at zero.

210
00:12:52,867 --> 00:12:54,935
And the rollout is stuck waiting.

211
00:12:54,935 --> 00:13:00,951
Because of max unavailable zero, Kubernetes will not remove an old pod until a new one is ready.

212
00:13:00,951 --> 00:13:02,831
That is why users see nothing.

213
00:13:04,567 --> 00:13:05,807
Describe the pod.

214
00:13:05,807 --> 00:13:09,894
The readiness probe goes to port 80, and the event says:

215
00:13:09,894 --> 00:13:11,281
connection refused.

216
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:13,543
Is it the application?

217
00:13:13,543 --> 00:13:14,787
The logs say no:

218
00:13:14,787 --> 00:13:18,673
nginx started normally, with its worker processes.

219
00:13:18,673 --> 00:13:20,538
The application is fine.

220
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:22,936
Is it the container?

221
00:13:22,936 --> 00:13:26,334
Exec into the pod and try both ports.

222
00:13:26,334 --> 00:13:27,069
Port 80:

223
00:13:27,069 --> 00:13:28,813
connection refused.

224
00:13:28,813 --> 00:13:29,732
Port 8080:

225
00:13:29,732 --> 00:13:30,007
ok.

226
00:13:30,007 --> 00:13:39,190
The container listens on 8080, as a non-root process must, because ports below 1024 need privileges.

227
00:13:39,733 --> 00:13:42,306
And the service explains the confusion:

228
00:13:42,306 --> 00:13:48,376
the service listens on port 80 and forwards to the container port named http, which is 8080.

229
00:13:48,376 --> 00:13:53,258
The probe talks to the container directly, so it needs the container port.

230
00:13:53,258 --> 00:13:54,380
Root cause found.

231
00:13:56,133 --> 00:13:57,834
And the load balancer level:

232
00:13:57,834 --> 00:14:03,059
the Ingress backends and the target group contain only the two old pods, both healthy.

233
00:14:03,059 --> 00:14:05,914
The broken pod never received a single request.

234
00:14:05,914 --> 00:14:08,405
That is the readiness gate doing its job.

235
00:14:08,967 --> 00:14:14,376
After five minutes, the pipeline's rollout check timed out, and the deploy job failed:

236
00:14:14,376 --> 00:14:17,333
a red run, telling the team something is wrong.

237
00:14:17,867 --> 00:14:18,437
The fix:

238
00:14:18,437 --> 00:14:19,934
git revert, and push.

239
00:14:19,934 --> 00:14:26,066
The pipeline goes green, and at no point during this incident did a user see an error.

240
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:29,492
Let us connect everything.

241
00:14:29,492 --> 00:14:32,421
Look at where security lives in this project:

242
00:14:32,421 --> 00:14:35,000
in the code, in the secrets check, in the dependencies.

243
00:14:36,533 --> 00:14:39,025
In the Dockerfile and in the Terraform.

244
00:14:39,567 --> 00:14:42,670
In the Kubernetes manifests and in the container image.

245
00:14:43,233 --> 00:14:45,294
In the SBOM and the signature:

246
00:14:45,294 --> 00:14:47,973
what is inside, and where it came from.

247
00:14:48,533 --> 00:14:52,433
In the identity of the pipeline, and in the deployment itself.

248
00:14:52,967 --> 00:14:53,935
And at runtime:

249
00:14:53,935 --> 00:14:58,585
Pod Security, network policies, and the cloud settings around the nodes.

250
00:14:59,133 --> 00:15:00,501
That is DevSecOps:

251
00:15:00,501 --> 00:15:04,223
development, security and operations as one flow.

252
00:15:04,223 --> 00:15:07,718
Security is not a final step after deployment.

253
00:15:07,718 --> 00:15:10,605
If it is last, it is already too late.

254
00:15:12,367 --> 00:15:16,276
Here is the final architecture, exactly as it ran.

255
00:15:16,276 --> 00:15:17,918
The developer pushes.

256
00:15:17,918 --> 00:15:23,781
GitHub Actions runs every check, builds, scans, creates the SBOM and signs.

257
00:15:24,333 --> 00:15:28,408
Through OIDC it pushes to ECR, by digest.

258
00:15:28,967 --> 00:15:30,494
In Frankfurt, the VPC:

259
00:15:30,494 --> 00:15:34,658
the load balancer and the NAT gateway in the public subnets.

260
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:39,128
The private nodes, the cluster with access entries and KMS,

261
00:15:39,128 --> 00:15:42,989
our restricted pods, and the controller with Pod Identity.

262
00:15:43,533 --> 00:15:46,343
And KMS, IAM and CloudWatch around it.

263
00:15:46,343 --> 00:15:52,333
From the developer's laptop to the public URL, every step has a security control.

264
00:15:54,067 --> 00:15:59,614
Let us be clear about what we built, and what a mature enterprise platform would add.

265
00:15:59,614 --> 00:16:02,355
On the left, what this project implements:

266
00:16:02,355 --> 00:16:05,227
the infrastructure as code with guard rails,

267
00:16:05,227 --> 00:16:08,359
private and encrypted nodes, keyless identities,

268
00:16:08,359 --> 00:16:12,079
scanning at every step, signed images deployed by digest,

269
00:16:12,079 --> 00:16:16,713
restricted pods, zero downtime rollouts, logs, and a verified teardown.

270
00:16:17,267 --> 00:16:19,422
On the right, the next steps.

271
00:16:19,422 --> 00:16:23,956
One NAT gateway per zone, Terraform state in S3 with locking,

272
00:16:23,956 --> 00:16:29,159
separate AWS accounts and environments with staging before production,

273
00:16:29,159 --> 00:16:34,065
and HTTPS with Route 53, a certificate from ACM, and WAF in front.

274
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:40,638
Secrets from Secrets Manager, admission policies that make signature verification mandatory,

275
00:16:40,638 --> 00:16:45,364
runtime security, central logging, Prometheus and Grafana with alerting,

276
00:16:45,364 --> 00:16:48,580
backups, disaster recovery and cost optimisation.

277
00:16:49,133 --> 00:16:50,470
And the process side:

278
00:16:50,470 --> 00:16:53,653
branch protection, required reviews and approvals,

279
00:16:53,653 --> 00:16:56,709
dependency updates, vulnerability fix deadlines,

280
00:16:56,709 --> 00:17:00,784
policy as code, a private API endpoint with self hosted runners,

281
00:17:00,784 --> 00:17:04,158
and multiple regions if the business really needs it.

282
00:17:05,900 --> 00:17:07,602
One explicit limitation:

283
00:17:07,602 --> 00:17:09,588
this lab runs on plain HTTP.

284
00:17:09,588 --> 00:17:12,850
A TLS certificate is issued for a domain name,

285
00:17:12,850 --> 00:17:18,950
and for this tutorial there is no domain, only the load balancer's AWS generated name.

286
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:24,565
In a real production environment, I would put the domain in Route 53,

287
00:17:24,565 --> 00:17:30,511
issue a certificate with ACM, add an HTTPS listener with two Ingress annotations,

288
00:17:30,511 --> 00:17:32,786
and redirect all HTTP to HTTPS.

289
00:17:33,333 --> 00:17:36,655
And for a public application this is not optional.

290
00:17:36,655 --> 00:17:41,703
Without TLS, anyone on the network path can read the traffic, and change it.

291
00:17:43,467 --> 00:17:44,116
And cost.

292
00:17:44,116 --> 00:17:47,146
Roughly, at on-demand prices in Frankfurt:

293
00:17:47,146 --> 00:17:50,392
ten cents per hour for the EKS control plane,

294
00:17:50,392 --> 00:17:54,287
ten cents for the two nodes, five for the NAT gateway,

295
00:17:54,287 --> 00:18:00,347
and about five more for the load balancer, public IP addresses, disks, KMS and logs.

296
00:18:00,347 --> 00:18:03,088
Around thirty cents per hour in total.

297
00:18:03,633 --> 00:18:08,098
So a five to eight hour training day costs about two to three dollars.

298
00:18:08,098 --> 00:18:10,330
Keep a safety buffer of around ten.

299
00:18:10,330 --> 00:18:11,606
These are estimates:

300
00:18:11,606 --> 00:18:16,390
prices change, and your own bill is the truth, so check it in your account.

301
00:18:16,933 --> 00:18:18,999
To make it cheaper for learning:

302
00:18:18,999 --> 00:18:24,423
one node instead of two, spot instances, and above all, delete the lab the same day.

303
00:18:24,423 --> 00:18:28,943
Or practise the Kubernetes parts locally first, with kind or minikube.

304
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:32,914
Now the part most tutorials skip:

305
00:18:32,914 --> 00:18:34,255
deleting everything.

306
00:18:34,255 --> 00:18:40,695
Before destroying anything, let us make sure we only delete resources belonging to this project,

307
00:18:40,695 --> 00:18:42,238
and in the right order.

308
00:18:42,238 --> 00:18:44,116
First, stop new deployments.

309
00:18:44,667 --> 00:18:45,251
Step one:

310
00:18:45,251 --> 00:18:47,591
delete the Kubernetes Ingress first.

311
00:18:47,591 --> 00:18:50,515
The load balancer controller created the ALB,

312
00:18:50,515 --> 00:18:55,519
so the controller must remove it, with its target groups and security groups.

313
00:18:55,519 --> 00:19:00,652
If you run terraform destroy first, the controller dies before it can clean up,

314
00:19:00,652 --> 00:19:03,771
the ALB is orphaned, and deleting the VPC fails.

315
00:19:04,333 --> 00:19:04,963
Step two:

316
00:19:04,963 --> 00:19:06,221
terraform destroy.

317
00:19:06,221 --> 00:19:12,583
That includes the container registry, which has force delete set, so its images go with it.

318
00:19:13,133 --> 00:19:13,897
Step three:

319
00:19:13,897 --> 00:19:16,537
the KMS key is scheduled for deletion.

320
00:19:16,537 --> 00:19:22,650
AWS enforces a waiting period of at least seven days, so you can recover from a mistake.

321
00:19:22,650 --> 00:19:25,498
A key waiting for deletion costs nothing.

322
00:19:26,033 --> 00:19:27,059
And step four:

323
00:19:27,059 --> 00:19:27,572
verify.

324
00:19:27,572 --> 00:19:32,628
The teardown script does all of this, and then runs the verification.

325
00:19:34,367 --> 00:19:36,143
Deploys off, then the script.

326
00:19:36,143 --> 00:19:42,513
The Ingress is deleted, and the script waits until the load balancer is really gone before it continues.

327
00:19:43,067 --> 00:19:44,531
Then terraform destroy:

328
00:19:44,531 --> 00:19:49,749
the NAT gateway in one minute, the cluster in two and a half, and finally the VPC.

329
00:19:49,749 --> 00:19:51,786
Seventy two resources destroyed.

330
00:19:53,533 --> 00:19:56,724
Do not just say everything has been deleted.

331
00:19:56,724 --> 00:19:57,449
Verify it.

332
00:19:57,449 --> 00:20:01,726
The script checks every resource type this project creates:

333
00:20:01,726 --> 00:20:04,917
the cluster, the VPC, subnets, NAT gateways,

334
00:20:04,917 --> 00:20:09,050
IP addresses, nodes, network interfaces, security groups,

335
00:20:09,050 --> 00:20:12,965
the load balancer and its target groups, the registry,

336
00:20:12,965 --> 00:20:18,258
the log groups, the KMS alias, and anything else tagged with our project.

337
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:20,405
Then IAM, which is global:

338
00:20:20,405 --> 00:20:22,381
our roles and policies are gone.

339
00:20:22,381 --> 00:20:25,961
They are matched by name and confirmed by the project tag,

340
00:20:25,961 --> 00:20:30,530
so another team's similarly named role can never be touched or miscounted.

341
00:20:31,067 --> 00:20:34,631
And the shared GitHub identity provider still exists.

342
00:20:34,631 --> 00:20:37,791
We did not modify it, and we did not delete it.

343
00:20:38,333 --> 00:20:41,203
The KMS key is waiting out its seven days.

344
00:20:41,203 --> 00:20:41,614
Clean.

345
00:20:41,614 --> 00:20:43,459
And one more honest detail:

346
00:20:43,459 --> 00:20:50,907
the first time I ran this, it reported two deleted subnets as left over, because AWS's tag index lags behind.

347
00:20:50,907 --> 00:20:54,256
The script now checks each resource's real state,

348
00:20:54,256 --> 00:20:58,629
and it still counts anything it does not recognise as left over.

349
00:20:58,629 --> 00:21:00,338
When in doubt, fail safe.

350
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:05,337
And the final comparison with the inventory from part one:

351
00:21:05,337 --> 00:21:10,250
every number is back to where it started, and the shared identity provider is untouched.

352
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:12,120
The console agrees:

353
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:14,273
no clusters, no load balancers.

354
00:21:14,273 --> 00:21:20,665
We worked inside a controlled scope, and we only touched resources created for this project.

355
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:28,243
Let me repeat the rules I followed the whole time, because they matter more than any tool.

356
00:21:28,243 --> 00:21:30,450
One region, enforced by Terraform.

357
00:21:30,450 --> 00:21:35,450
Every resource named eks devsecops and tagged with the project and the owner.

358
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:41,001
In IAM, only uniquely named project roles, and never anything unrelated.

359
00:21:41,001 --> 00:21:44,961
The shared GitHub identity provider was reused read only:

360
00:21:44,961 --> 00:21:47,044
never modified, never deleted.

361
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:49,818
Always read the plan before apply.

362
00:21:49,818 --> 00:21:51,057
And leave no trace:

363
00:21:51,057 --> 00:21:56,406
tear down the same day, and verify it against the inventory you took at the start.

364
00:21:56,406 --> 00:21:58,623
We work inside a controlled scope.

365
00:21:58,623 --> 00:22:01,820
We only touch resources created for this project.

366
00:22:03,567 --> 00:22:05,295
So, what can you do now?

367
00:22:05,295 --> 00:22:09,256
Build an AWS network and an EKS cluster with Terraform,

368
00:22:09,256 --> 00:22:16,026
with private nodes, secure IAM, access entries, Pod Identity and the load balancer controller.

369
00:22:16,567 --> 00:22:19,654
Build a container, scan it, create its SBOM,

370
00:22:19,654 --> 00:22:25,336
sign it, and push it to a registry with immutable tags, then deploy it by digest.

371
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:29,616
Run a DevSecOps pipeline with OIDC, scanning the code,

372
00:22:29,616 --> 00:22:32,920
the dependencies, the Dockerfile, the Terraform,

373
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:37,324
the Kubernetes files and the image, and deploying automatically.

374
00:22:37,867 --> 00:22:42,247
Secure Kubernetes workloads, expose them through an AWS load balancer,

375
00:22:42,247 --> 00:22:45,376
and troubleshoot a failing rollout layer by layer.

376
00:22:45,933 --> 00:22:50,335
And destroy the whole environment safely, and prove that nothing is left.

377
00:22:50,900 --> 00:22:55,851
Everything is in the repository, with a study guide and hands-on labs.

378
00:22:55,851 --> 00:22:59,528
You didn't just watch someone explain EKS DevSecOps.

379
00:22:59,528 --> 00:23:05,681
You watched it get built, shipped, broken, fixed, and cleaned up on a real AWS account.

380
00:23:05,681 --> 00:23:07,237
Now build it yourself.

381
00:23:07,237 --> 00:23:08,651
Thanks for watching.
